    "created_at": "2025-03-12T07:59:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-03-12T07:59:57Z",
    "body": "Thank you for filing this. I agree, the current spec doesn't specify the given URL is special or not and this should be explicit. Or even the current spec may be wrong because the default value of [scheme](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-scheme) is empty string, which is not a [special scheme](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#special-scheme) so the URL wouldn't be [special](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#is-special).\n\n> Looking at WPT tests and Chrome results, the expected behaviour is to consider that the url is special and hostname is not considered opaque.\n\nYes.\n\n> Ideally, the url given to the basic URL Parser should be an URL built by a URL parser.\nMaybe that is what the spec should do.\n\nAre you referring [URL parser](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-parser)? I see there is a comment in [URL parser](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-parser), saying _\"Non-web-browser implementations only need to implement the basic URL parser\"_. I assume some non-web-browser implementers don't implement this algorithm. Do you have any concern about just using the result of [basic URL parser](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-basic-url-parser), given \"http://dummy.test\" as an input and pass it as _dummyURL_ in https://urlpattern.spec.whatwg.org/#canonicalize-a-hostname?",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
